{
  "compilerOptions": {
    "target": "ES2022",
    "lib": ["ES2022", "DOM", "DOM.Iterable"],
    "module": "ESNext",
    "moduleResolution": "bundler",
    "types": ["vite/client"],

    "strict": true,
    /* The port from JavaScript was a rename, not a rewrite: the modules came
       across unchanged so that the frozen references still measure the same
       code. One strictness flag is what that still costs, rather than two.

       **strictNullChecks came on 2026-08-29**, and the 64 errors it had been
       hiding were worth the session. Most were not null-safety at all: an
       empty `const written = []` infers `never[]` under the flag, so every
       later read of it asks for a property on `never` - store.ts, voices.ts
       and eleven places in obf.ts were that, and the repair is to say what the
       collection holds. A handful were real: `canvas.getContext("2d")` and
       `canvas.toBlob()` can each answer null and neither was checked, and
       `input.files` is a FileList *or null* in two places that read [0] off it
       straight away.

       noImplicitAny is still off, and that is a decision rather than what is
       left over. Measured the same day: 116 errors on its own, 130 with both -
       and **70 of the 116 are in data/obf.ts**, the OBF/OBZ reader and writer
       held against the frozen references. Annotating that file is the highest
       risk and the lowest reward in the repository, because the locks prove
       behaviour and a wrong annotation there would be invisible to them.

       What that costs, stated plainly: an `any` is not null-checked either, so
       the guarantee this flag now gives is real but partial. It is the right
       half to have done first.

       tsconfig.e2e.json and tsconfig.test.json carry the same pair and moved
       with this file. */
    "noImplicitAny": false,
    "strictNullChecks": true,

    "noUnusedLocals": true,
    "noUnusedParameters": true,
    "noFallthroughCasesInSwitch": true,

    "isolatedModules": true,
    "verbatimModuleSyntax": true,
    "resolveJsonModule": true,
    "skipLibCheck": true,
    "noEmit": true,
    "composite": true,
    "tsBuildInfoFile": "./node_modules/.tmp/tsconfig.app.tsbuildinfo"
  },
  /* One program's worth of source, and that is what the split left.

     `loader` stood here too - the page that takes an exported file to a talker
     - along with `loader/tools/cable.js`, which needed allowJs above because
     it is JavaScript on purpose. adr/0012 took both to
     Lautstark/vorlaut-diy-talker, where the firmware they are held against
     already was. `third_party/` is deliberately absent and must stay absent:
     it holds a whole checkout of that repository for device/fixtures/, and a
     compiler that reads code out of it is the one edit that would quietly
     reinstate the dependency adr/0011 removed. */
  "include": ["src"]
}
